WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.062
Hash partition join is the workhorse for equality joins on huge tables.

00:00:07.062 --> 00:00:12.124
But it loses in one case.

00:00:12.124 --> 00:00:17.186
When the data is already sorted on the join key, or the next operator needs sorted output anyway, this algorithm wins instead.

00:00:17.186 --> 00:00:21.426
Phase one already ran.

00:00:21.426 --> 00:00:25.665
BigSort left both tables sorted on user id, sitting on disk.

00:00:25.665 --> 00:00:29.905
Now phase two, the merge.

00:00:29.905 --> 00:00:34.145
Two pointers, both sorted on disk, one pointer on each run.

00:00:34.145 --> 00:00:38.385
Compare the keys under the pointers.

00:00:38.385 --> 00:00:42.624
If the left key is smaller, advance left.

00:00:42.624 --> 00:00:46.864
If larger, advance right.

00:00:46.864 --> 00:00:51.104
When they are equal you have a match, so emit every pair in that key group, then move both past it.

00:00:51.104 --> 00:00:55.343
Mickey's Users row meets Mickey's Listens rows at the same point in the walk.

00:00:55.343 --> 00:00:59.583
Watch it run, two pointers crossing the runs in a single pass.

00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:03.823
Each key lines up, the dashed links fire, eight output rows stream out.

00:01:03.823 --> 00:01:08.062
One linear pass per run.

00:01:08.062 --> 00:01:12.302
P of R plus P of S plus OUT, on top of the two sort costs.

00:01:12.302 --> 00:01:16.542
The output comes out already sorted on the key, so a following ORDER BY pays nothing.

00:01:16.542 --> 00:01:20.782
The catch is the worst case.

00:01:20.782 --> 00:01:25.021
If one mega-popular song dominates both sides, the merge degenerates into a per-key cross-product.

00:01:25.021 --> 00:01:29.261
P of R times P of S, plus OUT, and OUT itself can blow up.

00:01:29.261 --> 00:01:33.093
And in code, two passes.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:36.924
Sort both tables, then walk them with two pointers, emitting matches as the keys line up.

00:01:36.924 --> 00:01:40.551
Three tables, same idea.

00:01:40.551 --> 00:01:44.179
Users, Listens, Songs.

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:47.806
Sort-merge join Users and Listens on user id.

00:01:47.806 --> 00:01:51.434
That result is already sorted, so feed it forward.

00:01:51.434 --> 00:01:55.061
Sort that result and Songs on song id.

00:01:55.061 --> 00:01:58.689
Sort-merge join again.

00:01:58.689 --> 00:02:02.316
The cost is one BigSort per input plus a linear merge per join.

00:02:02.316 --> 00:02:05.944
When the plan wants sorted output anyway, the sort cost amortizes across the whole chain.

00:02:05.944 --> 00:02:09.571
That is exactly when sort-merge beats hash partition join.

00:02:09.571 --> 00:02:15.274
Sort-merge join leaves the result already sorted on the join key, so the next operator that wants order pays nothing extra.

00:02:15.274 --> 00:02:20.977
Always carry the plus OUT term, because one dominant key turns the merge into a per-key cross-product.

00:02:20.977 --> 00:02:26.679
Storage, then three join strategies, block nested loop, hash partition, sort-merge.

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:32.382
We have stopped counting pages and started picking algorithms.

00:02:32.382 --> 00:02:38.085
Indexing next, then the optimizer chooses for you.
